<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Independent Social Democratic Party of Germany</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/1931">1931</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as USPD</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Independent Social Democratic Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> (USPD) was formed in 1917 by socialists who broke with the Social Democrats over support for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. It was prominent in the revolution of 1918–1919 and in council governments such as that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>. In 1920 most of its members joined the Communists, and the rest rejoined the Social Democrats in 1922.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/independent-social-democratic-party-of-germany.jpg" alt="Independent Social Democratic Party of Germany" width="320" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dahn · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:USPD_logo,_1920.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Social Democratic Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> (SPD) officially supported the war bonds for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> in 1914. Fourteen of its 110 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> members, among them Karl Liebknecht and Hugo Haase, had spoken against them but bowed to party discipline. In December 1914 Liebknecht was the only member to vote against further bonds, and in December 1915 eighteen members voted with him and Otto Rühle. Liebknecht was expelled from the parliamentary group on 12 January 1916 and the eighteen on 24 March; they formed the Social Democratic Working Group. On 18 January 1917 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-ebert">Friedrich Ebert</a> had the group and the Spartacus League expelled from the party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The USPD was founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> from 6 to 8 April 1917, with 124 delegates. Hugo Haase and Georg Ledebour were elected chairmen. It was heterogeneous, uniting traditionalists such as Haase, revisionist opponents of the war such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurt-eisner">Kurt Eisner</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eduard-bernstein">Eduard Bernstein</a>, the theorist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-kautsky">Karl Kautsky</a>, and the Spartacus Group, which joined as a bloc on condition of complete political independence. The party agitated against the war and welcomed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a>. In July 1917 it voted against the Reichstag Peace Resolution as ambiguous.</p><p>When the revolution broke out in 1918, the USPD leadership was caught off guard. After the Majority Social Democrats offered a joint government, USPD members joined the Council of the People's Deputies but resigned after less than two months, as the USPD favoured a council republic and the SPD a parliamentary one. In the election of 19 January 1919 it won only 7.6 percent of the vote. At a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> congress in November 1919 it adopted a programme calling for political workers' councils, with Ernst Däumig and Arthur Crispien as chairmen.</p><p>In October 1920 a party convention at Halle voted 236 to 156 to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Comintern</a>. The party split, and on 4 December 1920 the Left USPD, with about 400,000 members, merged into the Communist Party, while the Right USPD, with about 340,000, continued. After the murder of Walther Rathenau in June 1922 the remainder rejoined the SPD that September, though a shrunken USPD continued until 1931.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Independent Social Democratic Party of Germany” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A revolutionary cabinet of the MSPD and <b>USPD</b>, chaired jointly by Hirsch and Heinrich Ströbel, confiscated the royal property and abolished the House of Lords on 15 November.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-state-of-prussia">Free State of Prussia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A Council of the People's Deputies of three MSPD and three <b>USPD</b> members governed until January 1919.</blockquote><a href="/empire/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The council government, dominated by Communists and <b>Independent Socialists</b>, controlled the city for less than a month.</blockquote><a href="/state/bremen-soviet-republic">Bremen Soviet Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>In November 1918 King William II abdicated, and a provisional government of Social Democrats and <b>Independents</b> declared a republic in Stuttgart.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-peoples-state-of-wurttemberg">Free People's State of Württemberg</a></li><li><blockquote>On 7 November 1918 the <b>Independent Socialist</b> Kurt Eisner proclaimed the Free State of Bavaria in Munich, ending the Wittelsbach monarchy.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-bavaria">Free State of Bavaria</a></li><li><blockquote>The state was industrial, with textile and machine-building works around Gera and Greiz, and its politics were dominated by the Social Democrats and <b>Independent Socialists</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/peoples-state-of-reuss">People's State of Reuss</a></li></ul></section>
